Although implementations may differ in some aspects (such as power), there
are soem common specifications for them.

It might be able to use ATX power, so the expansion and common hardware
components usable in different implementations can be made to use the
voltages specified for ATX power (+3.3, +5, +12, -12, and -5), and also
the colours if wires of ATX power.

The external connectors and drives can include:

  RS-232
  Ethernet
  DVD recorder
  Digi-RGB video out
  Digi-RGB video in
  Analog audio out
  Analog audio in
  Keyboard connector
  GPIO
  MIDI

(Maybe there should be some others as well, e.g. for printers, joystick)


=== Keyboard ===

There is a dedicated keyboard port. The mouse is connected through the
keyboard, and does not have its own connector on the computer. (The same
connector for the mouse could also be used for e.g. barcode scanner.)

It can use a protocol (in both directions) with ten bits. The high 2-bits
specify the type of code, which can be:

* ON: Indicates a key is pushed.

* OFF: Indicates a key is released.

* COMMAND: Commands other than pushed/released.

* DATA: Data for a command.

Some commands from the computer to the keyboard might be:

* Reset the keyboard and tell the keyboard to send the ON codes for all of
the keys that are currently pushed, in case some have been missed.

* Set lights.


=== Video ===

The video output is using Digi-RGB, which is one way video that has pins
for the bits of the RGB channels, as well a pixel clock and vblank, and
ground and power.

The aspect ratio must be 4:3, 5:4, 8:5, or 16:9; each of these has a
different squarefree core and that is how the display would know what
the aspect ratio is.

(The video signal can also be recorded.)

